<component name="ProjectRunConfigurationManager">
  <!--
    Runs the backend in the IDE. Committed on purpose: IntelliJ reads .run/ from
    the repository, unlike .idea/, which is gitignored - so everyone gets a
    working configuration instead of each person rediscovering the setup.

    Database
    --------
    Deliberately sets no DB_* variables. It uses the defaults in
    application.properties, which point at localhost:${DB_PORT:5432}. That is a
    PostgreSQL installed on the machine, holding talabaty_db - no container
    needed.

    To run against the Postgres container instead of a local install:

        docker compose up -d postgres

    then add the variables its .env publishes it under, for example
    DB_PORT=5433 and DB_PASSWORD=postgres. DB_PORT is enough to move the port,
    because application.properties derives the JDBC URL from it.

    Either way the container backend must be stopped first, or the two fight
    over port 8080:  docker compose stop backend

    OTP
    ---
    otp.enabled defaults to true, and signup then tries to send mail through
    SMTP that is not configured locally. Note this does not make signup usable:
    the account is still created unverified and login rejects it with 403, so
    register a user and you still cannot log in as them. Use a seeded account.
  -->
  <configuration name="TalabatyApplication" type="SpringBootApplicationConfigurationType"
                 factoryName="Spring Boot" nameIsGenerated="true">
    <module name="backend" />
    <option name="SPRING_BOOT_MAIN_CLASS" value="com.talabaty.backend.TalabatyApplication" />
    <envs>
      <env name="OTP_ENABLED" value="false" />

      <!--
        The account one-time passwords are sent from. An address is not a
        secret, so it lives here; the password must not.

        MAIL_PASSWORD stays empty in the committed file. It is a Google *app
        password* - 16 characters from myaccount.google.com/apppasswords, with
        2-Step Verification switched on; Gmail's SMTP refuses a normal account
        password. Fill it in your own IntelliJ copy and do not commit it.

        Order matters. Until MAIL_PASSWORD is set, leave OTP_ENABLED=false: a
        username with no password makes the app treat mail as configured and
        attempt a send that cannot authenticate, turning every signup into a
        500. With OTP_ENABLED=false it keeps logging the code instead, which is
        harmless. Set both together, then flip OTP_ENABLED to true.
      -->
      <env name="MAIL_USERNAME" value="20fares05@gmail.com" />
      <env name="MAIL_PASSWORD" value="" />
    </envs>
    <method v="2">
      <option name="Make" enabled="true" />
    </method>
  </configuration>
</component>
